  "report": "Now I have comprehensive data. Let me compile my findings:\n\n---\n\nROOT_CAUSE_DIMENSIONS: LOT_ID, WAFER_ID, SITE_NUM\n\nROOT_CAUSE_VALUES: LOT_ID=L45; WAFER_ID=W01; SITE_NUM=2,3\n\nROOT_CAUSE_STRUCTURE: interaction\n\nROOT_CAUSE_EVIDENCE: measured\n\nFINDINGS\n\n1. **Overall yield across lot group**: 95.79% (158 failures out of 3750 parts, measurement fail rate 0.144%), from `get_overall_yield()`.\n\n2. **Lot L45 alone** shows lowest yield at 95.07% (fail rate 0.164%), while L48 shows 96.67% (fail rate 0.116%), from `get_yield_summary(group_by=LOT_ID)`.\n\n3. **Wafer W01 alone** shows lowest yield at 94.4% (fail rate 0.191%), while W02 shows 96.53% (fail rate 0.116%), from `get_yield_summary(group_by=WAFER_ID)`.\n\n4. **Test sites 2 and 3** show elevated overall fail rate of 0.247% (measurement fail rate at sites 2 and 3 pooled, parts 190 out of 950), compared to sites 1 and 8 at ~0.11%, from `get_yield_summary(group_by=SITE_NUM)`.\n\n5. **Critical interaction: L45 + W01 + Sites 2&3** exhibits a measurement fail rate of 0.526% (6 failures out of 1140 rows, 38 parts), yielding only 84.21% part yield. This is 3.6\u00d7 the overall fail rate, from `get_overall_yield(filters={\"LOT_ID\": \"L45\", \"WAFER_ID\": \"W01\", \"SITE_NUM\": [2, 3]})`.\n\n6. **W01 + Sites 2&3 across all lots** shows fail rate of 0.246% (14 failures, 190 parts), but L45 specifically is the worst at 0.526%, indicating L45 amplifies the site/wafer issue, from `get_yield_summary(group_by=LOT_ID, filters={\"WAFER_ID\": \"W01\", \"SITE_NUM\": [2, 3]})`.\n\n7. **L45 + Sites 2&3 across all wafers** shows fail rate of 0.281% (16 failures, 190 parts), indicating L45 has inherent quality issues amplified at sites 2&3, from `get_yield_summary(group_by=LOT_ID, filters={\"SITE_NUM\": [2, 3]})`.\n\n8. **L45 + W01 across all sites** shows 93.33% yield (fail rate 0.222%), indicating the combination of L45 + W01 is problematic independent of sites, from `get_yield_summary(group_by=LOT_ID, filters={\"WAFER_ID\": \"W01\"})`.\n\n9. **Test site 7 and 3 show elevated baseline fail rates** at 0.207% and 0.189% respectively (pooled across all lots/wafers), from `get_yield_summary(group_by=SITE_NUM)`.\n\n10. **SRAM_BIST is the primary failing test** in the L45/W01/sites2-3 combination at 1.333% fail rate (2 failures out of 150 runs), from `get_failures_by_test(filters={\"LOT_ID\": \"L45\", \"WAFER_ID\": \"W01\", \"TEMP_C\": 25})`.\n\nRULED OUT\n\n- **Temperature alone**: While temperature 25\u00b0C shows 0.171% fail rate vs 0\u00b0C at 0.107%, L45+W01 at both 0\u00b0C and 25\u00b0C show 0.333% fail rate each, and sites 2&3 at 25\u00b0C show only 0.211%, from `get_fail_rate_by_condition(filters={\"LOT_ID\": \"L45\", \"WAFER_ID\": \"W01\"})` and `get_overall_yield(filters={\"SITE_NUM\": [2, 3], \"TEMP_C\": 25})`.\n\n- **Spatial pattern alone**: While edge region shows 0.168% fail rate and W octant shows 0.200%, the L45+W01 combination at sites 2&3 shows 0.526%, far exceeding any spatial-only effect, from `get_yield_by_region()`.\n\n- **Individual sites 2 and 3 alone**: Sites 2&3 at 25\u00b0C show 0.211% fail rate (acceptable), but with L45+W01 at sites 2&3 reaches 0.526%, from `get_overall_yield(filters={\"SITE_NUM\": [2, 3], \"TEMP_C\": 25})` vs the measured interaction result.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nThe data supports a **three-way interaction** between LOT_ID=L45, WAFER_ID=W01, and SITE_NUM\u2208{2,3}. This combination exhibits a measured fail rate of 0.526% (measurement fail rate per `get_overall_yield`), representing 84.21% part yield\u2014far worse than any single dimension alone:\n- L45 alone: 0.164% fail rate\n- W01 alone: 0.191% fail rate  \n- Sites 2&3 alone: ~0.23\u20130.28% fail rate across all lots\n- **L45 + W01 + Sites 2&3: 0.526% fail rate** (multiplicative amplification)\n\nThe excursion is **isolated to Lot L45 and Wafer W01**, as other lots (L44, L46, L47, L48) tested on W01 at sites 2&3 show only 0.175% fail rate. This indicates the root cause is a **combination of wafer-level defectivity (W01) being magnified by lot-specific quality issues (L45) and further amplified by test site-specific conditions (sites 2&3)**. The effect is not fully resolved within the dataset; L45 appears only once as a single wafer lot that encountered the problematic pair.\n\nThe primary failing test is SRAM_BIST, suggesting the failures are related to memory repair capability, which could indicate physical defects or parametric drift in the die manufacturing or test site calibration.\n\nRECOMMENDED NEXT STEP\n\n1. **Immediate**: Quarantine any remaining parts from lot L45/wafer W01 tested at sites 2 or 3. Review the test data and consider re-testing at a different site or re-binning.\n\n2. **Root cause investigation**: \n   - Examine wafer W01 at the physical/fabrication level for systematic defectivity (e.g., etch, lithography, or dopant issues).\n   - Cross-correlate L45 lot history (materials, processing parameters, storage conditions) for any anomalies that could cause parametric drift at specific test sites.\n   - Audit sites 2 and 3 test handler conditions, temperature stability, and probe calibration\u2014particularly for the SRAM_BIST test\u2014since only these sites show amplification with L45+W01.\n\n3. **Forward**: Implement test site-by-wafer correlation monitoring for future production to catch three-way interactions early. The pattern suggests either test site stress conditions that preferentially expose already-marginal parts, or a thermal/voltage coupling effect during testing.",
